</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Comparison to LLM-as-a-judge</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the third experiment, we compare our proposed approach to establish LLM-as-a-judge methods, which have recently been proposed as effective solution to the unjudged document problem. Furthermore, given the demonstrated benefits of topic specialization for judges, we investigate if establishing specific topic context through few-shot prompting increases LLM-as-judge effectiveness.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">How do judge adapters compare to LLM-as-a-judge?</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We sample 256 documents per topic as train set for all topics of DL20. These retain their ground truth relevance annotations, and are used both to train judge adapters, as well as sample 4 relevant and 4 non-relevant few-shot examples from. In a similar evaluation setup as in the previous experiment, we compare Spearmans’ <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> rank correlation between system rankings as induced by different depths of nDCG. Additionally, we calculate the overall agreement between each approach and the ground truth labels using Krippendorff’s <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> for nominal scales. <a href="#S5.T2" title="In Does domain shift influence judge reliability? ‣ 5.2 Model Judges from Shallow Relevance Judgments ‣ 5 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Topic-Specific Classifiers are Better Relevance Judges than Prompted LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> lists results. Judge adapters provide the best approximation of the original pool, exhibiting both highest correlation (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0.81" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.81</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0.81</annotation></semantics></math>) and agreement (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0.55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.55</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0.55</annotation></semantics></math>) values in all cases. With the exception of nDCG@50 for lower train set sizes, across all metrics, judge adapters outscore the LLM-as-a-judge approaches. Furthermore, judge adapter provide more stable correlation values throughout the different evaluation depths.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Does topic context through few-shot prompting increase LLM-as-judge effectiveness?</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing zero-shot and few-shot variants of LLM-as-a-judge in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In Does domain shift influence judge reliability? ‣ 5.2 Model Judges from Shallow Relevance Judgments ‣ 5 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Topic-Specific Classifiers are Better Relevance Judges than Prompted LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, in both cases, providing examples yields increased effectiveness. This provides further evidence for our underlying assumption that topic specialization is a favorable characteristic. Yet, few-shot prompting massively increases the token budget and thus computational cost needed for relevance label inference, while judge adapters have negligible inference cost in comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Practical Considerations for Using Judge Adapters</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To drive adoption of our approach within the wider research community, enabling the comparison of models on test collections that were previously not possible due to the retrieval of too many unjudged documents, we distribute code to train new judge adapters, as well as our existing judge adapters for DL19, DL20, and Robust04. However, we do so with the caveat that these models are only ever to be used for the specific use case of assessing unjudged documents for the intended topic. For practitioners looking to train judge adapters on new test collections, several considerations emerge from our experiments:</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments suggest that between 100 and 200 judged documents per topic are needed to yield highly reliable judge adapters.
The natural class imbalance in judgment pools (typically between 3-12% relevant documents) should be handled through adapting the loss function class weights to the specific test collection at hand. We found that oversampling strategies did not improve over loss weighting, and thus recommend it as the simpler approach.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Base Model.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The choice of base ranking model impacts both adapter effectiveness and computational requirements. Mono-decoders consistently outperformed other architectures in our experiments, with a justifiable increase in parameter budget. LoRA adapted monoT5 models are be able to be trained and ran for inference on most consumer GPUs, or even CPUs, in contrast to LLM-as-a-judge methods. Domain alignment between the base model’s pretraining corpus and the target test collection improves effectiveness, though cross-domain transfer remains feasible with slightly more training data to compensate for domain shift.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Advantages.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments suggest judge adapters are most advantageous when:
(1) a sufficient pool of existing judgments exists, as evaluation on relevance labels predicted by judge adapters provide more robust estimations of system effectiveness than alternative approaches;
(2) deterministic, reproducible, and/or explainable judgments are required, as judge adapters provide a discriminative approach to automatic relevance judgment; and
(3) scalability is required and/or inference cost are a concern, as LLM-as-a-judge approaches require significantly more computational resources, particularly with few-shot prompting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Judge adapters are designed specifically to extend existing judgment pools by approximating how a particular assessor would judge previously unseen documents for a specific topic. Thus, <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the assessment of similar topics or using judge adapters for distillation is invalid.</span> The inclusion of the models we have presented in this paper within a system in any way apart from judging whether a document is relevant to a topic or not at evaluation time constitutes the equivalent to ‘training on the test set’.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The assumption that unjudged documents are non-relevant can lead to unreliable and incomparable results, especially as retrieval model effectiveness becomes far better than systems that contributed to judgment pools. We address this through topic-specific judge adapters: LoRA-based finetuning of pretrained rankers that deliberately overfit to individual assessors’ relevance criteria for individual topics. Aligning the predictions of pre-trained ranking models with topic-specific judgments addresses key limitations of previous work, specificially LLM-as-a-judge, namely circularity, amplification of biases, and extreme resource usage. Instead, offer both a reliable and scalable approach to extend shallow pools to greater depth through automatic judgment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments across three test collections demonstrate that judge adapters trained on as few as 128 examples per topic consistently achieve Spearman’s <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0.87" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.87</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0.87</annotation></semantics></math> correlation with ground truth system rankings, substantially outperforming both the non-relevance assumption baseline and LLM-as-a-judge approaches. Judge adapters exhibit higher agreement with ground truth labels (Krippendorff’s <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.69" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.69</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.69</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.15</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.49</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.49</annotation></semantics></math> for LLMs), and avoid circularity by differing from ranking models through topic-specific weight adaptation. The use of LoRA enables lightweight distribution and deterministic evaluation at negligible inference cost. These results establish judge adapters as a practical solution that preserves human judgment as the gold standard while extending test collection utility. More broadly, our findings reveal that topic-specific alignment, capturing how a particular assessor judges a particular topic, is both feasible with limited training data and more effective than seeking general-purpose relevance classifiers or relying on LLMs’ pretrained relevance priors only.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Future Work</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ability to accurately encode human rater preferences about texts has benefits beyond being able to judge the relevance of an unjudged document in an information retrieval system without the involvement of the original human judge. Firstly, it enables the assessment of relevance from many more perspectives. We are able to very precisely model a human rater with relatively few judgments from them. However, relevance is a single dimension for evaluating the effectiveness of information retrieval systems. In many search scenarios dimensions like trustworthiness and understandability are key to evaluating effectiveness. We plan to investigate if the approach we developed in this paper can be used to model dimensions beyond relevance. Another major open problem facing the information retrieval community at the moment is the reliable offline evaluation of RAG responses, as these are generated at query time and thus not able to be evaluated within the Cranfield paradigm. Disregarding the evaluation of attribution and focusing exclusively on the textual content, we plan to investigate the same technique presented in this paper to evaluate the quality of RAG responses according to different utility dimensions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
